  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Emergent reported $362M in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date across 152 awards, with an average award size of $2.4M. Obligations are down 0.1% year over year. Fiscal-year trends show obligations of $470M in FY21, $518M in FY22, $520M in FY23, $396M in FY24, $362M in FY25, and $362M in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $102M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $70.6M and the Department of State with $49.3M. Other significant buyers include the Department of Homeland Security at $45.9M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $28.9M. Most FY2026 obligations are in Other Computer Related Services at $333M. Electronic Computer Manufacturing accounts for $27.2M, and Software Publishers appear with $1.6M and $342K entries. Smaller market entries include Computer and Computer Peripheral Equipment and Software Merchant Wholesalers at $94K and Computing Infrastructure Providers, Data Processing, Web Hosting, and Related Services at $60K.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Emergent's federal sales?",
        "body": "Other Computer Related Services is the dominant market with $333M in FY2026 to date. Electronic Computer Manufacturing is next at $27.2M, and Software Publishers appear with $1.6M and $342K entries. Emergent has 152 awards and an average award size of $2.4M, which shows a mix of mid-size awards concentrated in a single primary NAICS."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are Emergent's top customers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is Emergent's top buyer at $102M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $70.6M and the Department of State at $49.3M. The Department of Homeland Security and the Department of Health and Human Services obligate $45.9M and $28.9M respectively. Ten agencies account for the top FY2026 buying list down to the Department of the Interior at $4.5M."
      }
    ]
  },
